<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantion</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byzantion was an ancient Greek city on the European shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, founded by colonists from Megara. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> refounded it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the fourth century CE, making it the new capital of the Roman world. Its original name gave rise to the modern term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantion.jpg" alt="Byzantion" width="312" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Evil berry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_Byzantion.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The origin of the name is unknown. It has been suggested to be Thracian, perhaps from the personal name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a>, which means he-goat. Greek legend names Byzas as the leader of the Megarian colonists and founder of the city. The city was mainly a trading city because it stood at the only entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and it later conquered Chalcedon across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>. Its position, surrounded by water on almost all sides, made it highly defensible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a> of Megara founded the city after sailing northeast across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. The date is usually given as 667 BCE on the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. Constantine the Great favoured that dating and marked the city's 1,000th anniversary between 333 and 334. The Persian Empire took the city at the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>'s Scythian campaign in 513 BCE. In the Peloponnesian War Sparta took it in 411 BCE to cut off grain supplies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the Athenians retook it in 408 BCE.</p><p>After the city sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pescennius-niger">Pescennius Niger</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, Roman forces besieged it and badly damaged it in 196 CE. Severus rebuilt it, and it soon regained its prosperity. In 330 CE Constantine I refounded it as an imperial residence modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, known as Nova Roma and later as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. For centuries it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The Ottoman Turks conquered it on 29 May 1453.</p><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> was used for the Eastern Roman Empire as a whole only from 1555, when the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-wolf">Hieronymus Wolf</a> introduced it. Its inhabitants continued to call their state the empire of the Romans. From the 9th century the names Byzantius and Byzantinus were applied to the empire's gold coinage, which gave the English words besant, byzant and bezant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Byzantium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I's dedication of Constantinople on 11 May 330 CE, on the site of the ancient Greek colony of <b>Byzantion</b> at the crossing of the Bosphorus, is the conventional founding moment of what later scholars called the Byzantine Empire, though its inhabitants called themselves Romans and recognised no break…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ionian Revolt and two Persian invasions of Greece, the Allies defeated the forces of Xerxes I at the Battle of Plataea and the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE, then took Sestos and, in 478 BCE, <b>Byzantion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Paeonians, took Amphipolis, Pydna and Potidaea in 357–356 BCE, crushed Onomarchus at the Battle of Crocus Field in 352 BCE, destroyed Olynthos in 348 BCE and, after failed sieges of Perinthus and <b>Byzantion</b> in 340 BCE, defeated Athens and Thebes at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Constantine I dedicated the rebuilt Greek city of <b>Byzantion</b>, refounded in 324, and renamed it Constantinopolis, Constantine's City.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
